<h2>How Much Does Roof Replacement Cost in Austin?</h2>
<p>Price is driven by the size of the roof in squares (one square is 100 square feet), the pitch, the number of layers being torn off, how much decking needs replacing and the material you choose. As a general planning range, a standard architectural shingle roof on a 1,500 square foot single-story home often lands somewhere around $8,000–$12,000, with larger, steeper or more complex roofs and premium materials costing more. These are typical industry figures, not an Austin Roofing quote — your written estimate is based on your actual roof.</p>
<p>Use the calculator below for a planning number, and see <a href="/blog/how-much-does-it-cost-to-replace-a-roof/">how much it costs to replace a roof on a 1,500 sq ft house</a> for a full breakdown of what shows up on an invoice.</p>

<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Comparing quotes?</strong> Ask every roofer the same questions: Is the tear-off included? How much decking is included before extra charges apply? Is new flashing and drip edge included, or reused? What underlayment and ventilation are specified? The cheapest number often leaves one of these out.</p></div>

<h2>Permits, HOAs and Austin-Specific Considerations</h2>
<p>Re-roofing rules vary between the City of Austin and the surrounding cities in Travis, Williamson and Hays counties, and some jobs — especially those changing roofing materials or replacing structural decking — are more likely to need a permit and inspection. We check the requirements for your address before work begins. Our post on <a href="/blog/roof-replacement-permit-requirements/">roof replacement permit requirements</a> explains how the process generally works.</p>
<p>Many neighborhoods in Cedar Park, Leander, Round Rock and Pflugerville also have HOA rules on shingle color, profile or metal roofing. We provide product data sheets and color samples for your architectural review committee, so approval does not hold up your schedule. And on wooded lots in West Austin and the Hill Country, we pay extra attention to valleys and gutters that collect oak and cedar debris, and to fire ratings where wildland-urban interface rules apply.</p>

<h2>Insurance-Funded Roof Replacements</h2>
<p>Spring hail season is the most common reason Austin homeowners end up with a new roof sooner than planned. If a storm has damaged your roof, we inspect it for free, document the damage with dated photos and can meet your insurance adjuster on site to point out what we found. We are a roofing contractor, not a public adjuster — your claim is between you and your insurer — but clear documentation helps the adjuster see the full picture. Learn more on our <a href="/services/roof-insurance-claims/">roof insurance claim help</a> page.</p>

